+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is handed verbatim to both providers, so the shape the model was told to send and the shape the engine accepts are the same document. This spec pins what the engine does with that document at call time: `ToolRegistry.execute` validates a call's `input` against the resolved tool definition's `inputSchema` before the executor runs, so a malformed call fails in one predictable way and no executor ever sees input the schema rejects.
+
+## What the registry enforces
+
+- A call whose `input` fails the schema is rejected inside `execute` before the executor runs: the executor is never invoked, and the registry returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the failing property and states the unmet constraint rather than throwing ([validated by: rejects a call missing a required property before the executor runs](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L64)).
+- A wrong-typed value is rejected with a message naming the property path and the expected type, and neither the returned text nor any log line contains the offending value, because tool input is where identifying data enters the engine ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed value, naming the path and the expected type but not the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L90)).
+- Valid input reaches the executor unchanged: properties the schema does not declare are passed through as-is, no defaults are injected, no types are coerced, and the executor is called exactly once ([validated by: passes valid input through to the executor unchanged, extra properties included](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L77)).
+- Validation enforces `required`, declared types and `enum` only: it does not inject `additionalProperties: false`, coerce types, apply defaults, or offer an opt-out flag.
+
+## When schemas are compiled
+
+- A static `ToolDefinition`'s schema is compiled once at `register` time, so an uncompilable schema fails at startup with an error naming the tool ([validated by: refuses at register time a tool whose schema cannot compile, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L121)).
+- A static tool's compiled schema is reused across every `execute` call that follows rather than recompiled per call.
+- A schema produced by a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves per call, so a call is validated against the schema current at that call rather than a stale cached one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at that call](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L142)).
+
+## What a rejection is
+
+- A rejected call is a returned `ToolResponse`, not an exception, so the model reads the rejection as a tool result and can correct the call within the existing tool-round budget ([validated by: feeds a rejected call back to the provider and completes on the corrected retry](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L182)).
+- The tool-round bound (`DEFAULT_MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS = 5`) is what stops a reject-and-retry cycle from spinning: no counter inside the registry ends the loop.
+- An unregistered tool name still throws `Unknown tool: <name>`, unchanged by validation ([validated by: still throws for a tool name that was never registered](../../src/orchestration/tools/toolInputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L171)).
